#!RuleTest
#test PMDA-SD1464 expect=noViolation domain=EX
#note "None of these EXDOSTXT values is numeric, so the rule is silent. ⚠⚠ Rewritten 2026-09-14, and NOT by re-pinning the count. This file used to carry '+1' and '1e5' and justified them with 'per the strict is_numeric scan' — under the parse-based is_numeric of engine ef60470 both are numeric, so the file's own premise became false and it failed with 2 violations. Re-pinning it to expect=violation would have left a scenario named valid_textual_dose asserting the opposite of its name, so the two rows MOVED to -invalid_numeric_dose_text (which already held '1e5') and are pinned there as firing rows. Nothing about the boundary stopped being covered; it is covered where it belongs. What is left here is dose text that is genuinely textual under any grammar — a word, a number with a unit, and an empty cell — which is what this half of the pair was for."
#library standard=sdtmig version=3-4
#expectViolationCount 0
dataset EX class=INTERVENTIONS label="Exposure"
col STUDYID  type=Char
col DOMAIN   type=Char
col USUBJID  type=Char
col EXSEQ    type=Num
col EXTRT    type=Char
col EXDOSTXT type=Char
---
S1 | EX | 001 | 1 | DRUGA | TWO TABLETS
S1 | EX | 001 | 2 | DRUGA | 500 mg
S1 | EX | 001 | 3 | DRUGA |
